2026-06-30 15:37:15,205 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-26e612da-b87e-4f37-8958-c14aa3b16d36', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nB102794_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-183-1.raw\nB102794_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-183-2.raw\nB102796_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-184-1.raw\nB102796_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-184-2.raw\nB102798_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-187-1.raw\nB102798_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-187-2.raw\nB102800_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-188-1.raw\nB102800_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-188-2.raw\nB102804_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-183-3.raw\nB102806_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-184-3.raw\nB102808_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-187-3.raw\nB102810_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-188-3.raw\nB102812_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-183-4.raw\nB102814_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-184-4.raw\nB102816_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-187-4.raw\nB102818_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-188-4.raw\nB102820_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-183-5.raw\nB102822_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-184-5.raw\nB102824_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-187-5.raw\nB102826_Ap_110uPAc_LumosLC_CMB-1046_SVEYC_AP-MS-188-5.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nLeveraging a self-cleaving peptide for tailored control in proximity labeling proteomics\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nPPIs play an important biological role in every aspect of cellular homeostasis and functioning. Proximity labeling mass spec-based proteomics overcomes challenges typically associated with other methods, and has quickly become the current state-of-the-art in the field. Nevertheless, tight control of proximity labeling enzymatic acitivty a,d expression levels is crucial to accurately identify protein interactors. Here, we leverage a T2A self-cleaving peptide and a non-cleavage mutant to allow the use of the POI in the experimental and control TurboID setup. To allow easy and streamlined plasmid assembly, we build a GOlden Gate modular cloning system to generate plasmids for transient expression and stable integration. To highlight our T2A split-link design, we applied it to identify PPIs of GR, NCAP, and NSP7 by TurboID proximity labeling proteomics. Our results demisntrate that our T2A split-link provides an opportune control that builds upon previously established control requirements in the field.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor each proximity labeling experiment, three 150 cm2 plates were seeded with 2.7 x 106 cells per replicate. Next day, optimized doxycycline concentrations as previously described were added to the corresponding setup. After 24 h, 50 uM biotin was added for 1 h. Cells were washed once on the plate with 10 mL ice-cold PBS, after wish cells were collected per replicate by scraping in 750 uL ice-cold PBS. Cells were pelleted by spinning for 5 min at 4°C at 500 x g. Pellets were washed once with 6 mL ice-cold PBS. Pellets were frozen at -20°C until further processing. Subsequently, cell pellets were resuspended in 5 mL ice-cold RIPA (50 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 150 mM NaCl, 1% NP-40, 2 mM EDTA, and 0.1% SDS in ddH2O), supplemented with 1X cOmplete Protease Inhibitor cocktail and 0.5% sodium deoxycholate. Next, 50 U mL-1 benzonase was added and samples were incubated for 1 h at 4°C with end-to-end rotation. Lysates were then sonicated on ice with a probe sonicated at 30% amplitude for 5 rounds of 6 s burst with 2 s in between rounds. Lysates were pelleted at 16100 x g for 15 min at 4°C and the supernatant was transferred to a new tube. Protein concentrations were determined by Bradford assay, and a maximal shared protein amount across all samples in the same experiment was used as an input for the affinity precipitation. Per replicate 30 uL Streptavidin Sepharose High Performance beads were washed three times with 600 uL unsupplemented ice-cold RIPA and eventually resuspended in 600 uL ice-cold supplemented RIPA buffer. Samples volumes were adjust to a minimum volume of 4.5 mL, and equilibrated beads were added to each sample. Affinity purification was performed by incubation at 4°C for 3 h with end-to-end rotation. Next, beads were pelleted by centrifugation for 1 min at 500 x g. Beads were washed with 1 mL unsupplemented RIPA buffer. This process was repeated for a total of three washes, after which beads were washed twice with freshly prepared ABC buffer (50 mM NH4HCO3 pH 8.0 in ddH2O). Next, beads were washed three times with trypsin digest buffer (20 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0 and 2 mM CaCl2 in ddH2O). Ultimately, beads were resuspended in 20 uL Tris-HCl pH 8.0, and 1 ug trypsin was added. Samples were incubated overnight at 37°C. Next morning, beads were pelleted and supernatant was transferred to a new tube. Another 0.5 ug trypsin was added and samples were incubated for an additional 3 h at 37°C. Peptide mixtures were acidified with 20% FA to a final concentration of 2% FA. Mixtures were centrifuged at 20000 x g for 10 min at room temperature, and the supernatant was transferred to a MS vial. Samples were frozen at -20°C until LC-MS/MS analysis. For each sample, 2.5 uL peptide mixture was injected for LC-MS/MS analysis on a Ultimate 3000 RSLCnano system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) in line connected to a Q-Exactive-HF Biopharma mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Trapping was performed 2O uL min-1 for 2 min in loading solvent A (98% ACN, 0.1% TFA) on a 5 mM trapping column (Thermi Fisher Scientific). Peptide separation was performed on a 250 mm Aurora Ultimate (IonOpticks) at a constant temperature of 45°C. Peptides were eluted by a non-linear gradient starting at 1% solvent B (80 ACN, 0.1% FA) reaching 33% solvent B in 60 min, 55% in 75 min, 70% in 90 min, followed by a wash at 70% solvent B for 10 min and re-equilibration with solvent A. The mass spectromter was operated in a data-dependent acquisition mode, automatically switching between MS1 and MS2 acquisition for the 12 most abundant ion peaks per MS1 spectrum. Full-scan MS spectra (375 – 1500 m/z) were acquired at a resolution of 60000 in the Orbitrap analyzer after accumulation to a target value of 3000000. The 12 most intense ions above a threshold of 15000 were isolated for fragmentation at a normalized collision energy of 30%. The C-trap was filled at a target value of 100000 for maximum 80 ms and the MS2 spectra (200 – 2000 m/z) were acquired at a resolution of 15000 in the Orbitrap analyzer with a fixed mass of 145 m/z. Only peptides with charge states ranging from +2 to +6 were included for fragmentation and the dynamic exclusion was set to 12 s.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
